specs.push(
q('coeliac-unexplained-iron-deficiency-screen','Extraintestinal coeliac presentation','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'coeliac','An adult has persistent iron-deficiency anemia despite replacement and no identified bleeding source. She reports bloating but no chronic diarrhea. Which additional condition warrants serologic assessment?',[
['Coeliac disease','Coeliac disease can present with unexplained iron deficiency without diarrhea.'],['Exclude all intestinal disease without diarrhea','Extraintestinal manifestations can occur without classic bowel symptoms.'],['Only acute pancreatitis','Pancreatitis does not best explain chronic unexplained iron deficiency.'],['Assume dietary adherence is the only possibility','Persistent deficiency deserves evaluation for impaired absorption and other causes.']], 'Coeliac disease may present through malabsorption-related iron deficiency rather than prominent diarrhea. Persistent unexplained anemia therefore justifies serologic assessment even when bowel symptoms are mild, rather than treating absence of diarrhea as an exclusion criterion.','Consider coeliac disease in unexplained persistent iron deficiency without diarrhea.','iron-deficiency anemia mechanism'),
q('coeliac-gluten-before-testing','Diet and diagnostic test validity','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'coeliac','A patient with suspected coeliac disease plans to stop all gluten today before blood tests and possible biopsy next month. Which advice best preserves diagnostic accuracy?',[
['Discuss continuing gluten during the diagnostic assessment','Removing gluten can reduce test sensitivity and obscure diagnosis.'],['A gluten-free diet improves every diagnostic test immediately','Restriction can normalize findings and make confirmation harder.'],['Symptoms alone always establish the diagnosis','Similar symptoms occur in several other digestive disorders.'],['Cancel assessment if symptoms improve on restriction','Diet response alone does not reliably establish coeliac disease.']], 'Coeliac serology and intestinal findings are influenced by gluten exposure. Starting a strict gluten-free diet before assessment can make results falsely reassuring, so testing should be planned with appropriate dietary advice rather than interpreting improvement as definitive proof.','Maintain appropriate gluten exposure while evaluating suspected coeliac disease.','intestinal absorption physiology'),
q('coeliac-iga-deficiency-serology','Coeliac serology with IgA deficiency','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'coeliac','An adult with strong clinical suspicion for coeliac disease has negative IgA tissue transglutaminase antibodies, but total IgA is markedly reduced. What is the most appropriate interpretation?',[
['IgA deficiency can make this serology falsely reassuring','An appropriate IgG-based pathway should be considered in IgA deficiency.'],['Coeliac disease is definitively excluded','Low IgA limits interpretation of a negative IgA-based assay.'],['The negative assay proves gluten is harmless in all settings','One limited test cannot establish that broad conclusion.'],['Low IgA directly proves pancreatic cancer','This result does not establish a pancreatic malignancy.']], 'An IgA-based coeliac assay depends on adequate IgA production. Markedly low total IgA makes a negative result less reliable and calls for an appropriate alternative testing strategy rather than exclusion of disease on that result alone.','Recognize the limitation of negative IgA coeliac testing in IgA deficiency.','immunoglobulin classes'),
q('coeliac-negative-test-high-suspicion','Persistent coeliac suspicion','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'coeliac','A patient eating gluten has negative coeliac serology but persistent malabsorptive symptoms and unexplained nutritional deficiencies. What is the best response if clinical suspicion remains high?',[
['Refer for specialist assessment despite the negative serology','Negative serology does not end evaluation when suspicion remains substantial.'],['State that every negative antibody test is infallible','Sensitivity is imperfect and context changes interpretation.'],['Diagnose irritable bowel syndrome without reviewing red flags','Nutritional deficiencies require assessment for an organic cause.'],['Treat the antibody result as proof of deliberate symptoms','A negative test cannot establish intentional symptom production.']], 'Serology is an important part of coeliac assessment but does not exclude every case. Persistent malabsorption and objective nutritional problems sustain clinical concern, supporting specialist evaluation rather than automatic reassurance or a functional label.','Continue specialist evaluation when coeliac suspicion remains high despite negative serology.','intestinal malabsorption'),
q('coeliac-persistent-symptoms-diet-review','Symptoms after coeliac diagnosis','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'coeliac','A patient with confirmed coeliac disease continues to have bloating after starting a gluten-free diet. What should be reviewed before assuming refractory disease?',[
['Dietary gluten exposure and alternative or coexisting causes','Persistent symptoms can reflect inadvertent gluten intake or another disorder.'],['Assume all persistent symptoms prove intestinal lymphoma','A serious complication is not the default explanation for every symptom.'],['Escalate immunosuppression without reassessment','Treatment escalation should not bypass dietary and diagnostic review.'],['Conclude that the original diagnosis is always wrong','Persistent symptoms do not automatically invalidate an established diagnosis.']], 'Persistent symptoms after coeliac diagnosis warrant review of adherence, inadvertent gluten exposure and other causes such as lactose intolerance or IBS. Refractory disease and complications require assessment when appropriate, but should not be assumed before these common explanations are examined.','Review exposure and coexisting disease before labeling coeliac symptoms refractory.','intestinal malabsorption'),
q('ibs-pain-bowel-association','Positive IBS symptom pattern','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'ibs','A young adult has recurrent abdominal pain associated with altered stool frequency and relief after defecation. Weight is stable, inflammatory markers are normal and there is no bleeding. Which diagnosis best fits?',[
['Irritable bowel syndrome','Pain related to bowel habit with reassuring assessment supports IBS.'],['Acute bowel perforation','A stable recurrent course does not fit acute peritonitis.'],['Acute pancreatitis','Pain linked to defecation and altered stool form is a different pattern.'],['Mechanical bowel obstruction in every episode','The chronic fluctuating pattern lacks obstructive red flags.']], 'IBS is supported by recurrent pain related to defecation or changes in stool frequency or form after appropriate assessment. Stable weight and absence of bleeding or inflammation make an organic inflammatory process less evident, while new red flags would require reassessment.','Identify an IBS-compatible pattern after appropriate red-flag assessment.','inflammatory bowel pattern'),
q('ibs-redflag-anemia-weightloss','Reassessing a functional bowel label','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'ibs','A patient previously labeled with IBS develops unexplained weight loss, iron-deficiency anemia and persistent nocturnal diarrhea. What should happen next?',[
['Reassess for an organic gastrointestinal cause','New alarm features warrant investigation beyond the prior IBS label.'],['Assume these findings are routine IBS','Anemia and weight loss are not explained by a functional label alone.'],['Only intensify reassurance indefinitely','Reassurance should not replace assessment of objective red flags.'],['Exclude all disease because an old colonoscopy was normal','A past result does not exclude a new or evolving disorder.']], 'An existing IBS diagnosis does not protect against later organic disease. New weight loss, anemia and nocturnal diarrhea change the clinical picture and justify investigation rather than diagnostic anchoring or escalation of symptom treatment alone.','Reevaluate IBS when objective alarm features develop.','inflammatory bowel pattern'),
q('ibs-baseline-exclusion-tests','Targeted initial tests for IBS','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'ibs','A patient meets an IBS symptom pattern without alarm features. Which initial test group is appropriate to assess common alternative diagnoses before proceeding with symptom-directed care?',[
['Full blood count inflammatory markers and coeliac serology','These tests evaluate anemia inflammation and coeliac disease.'],['Routine brain MRI and EEG','These do not evaluate common gastrointestinal alternatives to IBS.'],['No assessment of anemia or inflammation is ever needed','A positive symptom pattern still requires an appropriate basic assessment.'],['Mandatory exploratory laparotomy','Invasive surgery is not a routine investigation for this low-risk pattern.']], 'Guidance supports targeted basic testing such as a full blood count, inflammatory markers and coeliac serology in an IBS-compatible presentation. This assesses important alternatives without assuming that every low-risk patient requires an extensive invasive workup.','Select targeted basic tests in an IBS-compatible presentation without alarm features.','diagnostic test selection'),
q('ibs-no-routine-colonoscopy-lowrisk','Avoiding unnecessary IBS investigations','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'ibs','A 28-year-old has a typical IBS symptom pattern, reassuring examination and basic tests, and no alarm features or separate screening indication. Is colonoscopy required solely to confirm IBS?',[
['No routine colonoscopy is required solely for confirmation','A positive clinical diagnosis can follow suitable assessment in this context.'],['Yes because IBS can only be diagnosed by biopsy','There is no specific biopsy finding required to establish typical IBS.'],['Yes before any dietary discussion in every adult','Investigation should reflect risk rather than an automatic universal rule.'],['No future red flag should ever prompt colonoscopy','New symptoms or screening indications may change the need for investigation.']], 'A typical low-risk IBS presentation can be diagnosed positively after appropriate evaluation. Colonoscopy is not routinely required simply to confirm it, while new alarm features or independent age- and risk-based screening needs remain reasons to reconsider investigation.','Avoid routine colonoscopy solely to confirm a low-risk IBS presentation.','diagnostic testing stewardship'),
q('ibs-soluble-insoluble-fiber','Fiber choice in IBS','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'ibs','A patient with constipation-predominant IBS develops more bloating after adding large amounts of bran. If fiber is adjusted, which option is generally preferable?',[
['A trial of soluble fiber such as ispaghula','Soluble fiber may be better tolerated than insoluble bran in IBS.'],['Automatically double insoluble bran indefinitely','More bran can worsen bloating and is not the preferred response here.'],['Eliminate every source of nutrition','Severe indiscriminate restriction is not an appropriate fiber strategy.'],['Ignore the symptom response to diet','Dietary advice should account for tolerability and individual response.']], 'Insoluble fiber such as bran can aggravate IBS symptoms in some patients. If increasing fiber, soluble preparations such as ispaghula can be discussed with gradual adjustment and attention to tolerability rather than uncritically escalating the product causing bloating.','Choose soluble fiber rather than escalating poorly tolerated bran in IBS.','intestinal motility'),
q('pancreatitis-antibiotics-uninfected','Antibiotics in acute pancreatitis','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'pancreas','A patient has acute pancreatitis without evidence of infection, cholangitis or another bacterial focus. A trainee suggests antibiotics solely to prevent pancreatic infection. What is the best response?',[
['Do not routinely give prophylactic antibiotics','Uninfected acute pancreatitis is not itself a prophylactic antibiotic indication.'],['Give antibiotics to every pancreatitis patient indefinitely','Routine prophylaxis is not recommended and adds treatment harms.'],['Assume elevated lipase proves bacterial infection','Lipase elevation reflects pancreatic injury rather than a bacterial diagnosis.'],['Avoid reassessment if infection later develops','A new infectious complication changes the management indication.']], 'Acute pancreatitis does not automatically imply bacterial infection. Prophylactic antibiotics are not routinely recommended when infection is absent; antibiotics remain appropriate when a separate infection or infected complication is identified, which requires ongoing clinical reassessment.','Avoid routine prophylactic antibiotics for uninfected acute pancreatitis.','acute pancreatitis diagnostic criteria'),
q('pancreatitis-oral-intake-tolerated','Nutrition in mild pancreatitis','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'pancreas','A patient with mild acute pancreatitis is improving, no longer vomiting and able to tolerate food. Is prolonged fasting required solely until serum lipase becomes normal?',[
['No food need not be withheld without a clear clinical reason','Tolerated nutrition is not contingent on complete enzyme normalization.'],['Yes every patient must fast for two weeks','A fixed prolonged fast is not required for improving uncomplicated disease.'],['Parenteral nutrition is mandatory for every mild episode','An intravenous route is unnecessary when oral intake is suitable.'],['Lipase must rise before feeding can start','A rising enzyme level is not a prerequisite for nutrition.']], 'Improving patients should not be kept without food solely because lipase remains elevated. Clinical tolerance and clear contraindications guide nutrition; unnecessary fasting can impair recovery, while severe disease or persistent vomiting may require a different route and plan.','Avoid prolonged fasting solely for persistent enzyme elevation in improving pancreatitis.','acute pancreatitis diagnostic criteria'),
q('severe-pancreatitis-enteral-route','Nutrition route in severe pancreatitis','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'pancreas','A patient with severe pancreatitis cannot meet nutritional needs orally, but the gastrointestinal tract is usable. Which route should generally be prioritized over routine parenteral nutrition?',[
['Enteral nutrition','A functioning gastrointestinal tract supports enteral feeding in severe pancreatitis.'],['Parenteral nutrition regardless of gut function','Parenteral feeding is generally reserved for failed or contraindicated enteral care.'],['No nutrition until hospital discharge','Severe illness does not remove nutritional requirements.'],['Only sweetened water indefinitely','This cannot meet the nutritional needs of severe pancreatitis.']], 'Severe pancreatitis often requires nutritional support, and enteral nutrition is preferred when the gut can be used. Parenteral nutrition is considered when enteral feeding fails or is contraindicated, rather than serving as the automatic first choice for all severe episodes.','Prioritize enteral feeding when severe pancreatitis prevents adequate oral intake.','intestinal nutrition physiology'),
q('pancreatitis-nongallstone-causes','Etiological evaluation of pancreatitis','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'pancreas','An adult has confirmed pancreatitis, denies alcohol use and has no gallstones on initial assessment. What is an appropriate next etiological review?',[
['Review triglycerides calcium medicines and other plausible causes','Pancreatitis has metabolic drug-related and structural causes beyond alcohol and stones.'],['Conclude that pancreatitis is impossible','Absence of two common causes does not exclude an established episode.'],['Attribute it to alcohol despite contrary evidence','An etiological assessment should not presume undisclosed drinking without evidence.'],['Ignore etiology once pain improves','Cause identification may prevent recurrence and reveal treatable disease.']], 'Alcohol and gallstones are common causes, but their absence does not end the etiological workup. Metabolic abnormalities, medicines and structural or other causes merit evaluation according to the clinical setting, particularly to reduce recurrence and identify treatable contributors.','Investigate alternative causes of pancreatitis when alcohol and gallstones are absent.','acute pancreatitis diagnostic criteria'),
q('chronic-pancreatitis-malabsorption','Nutritional consequences of chronic pancreatitis','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'pancreas','A man with chronic pancreatitis has oily stools, weight loss and reduced intake. What clinical issue should be actively assessed alongside his pain?',[
['Malabsorption and malnutrition','Exocrine dysfunction can impair digestion and nutritional status.'],['Assume weight loss is desirable in every patient','Unintentional loss with steatorrhea signals nutritional risk.'],['Ignore diet until pain completely resolves','Nutritional assessment should not be postponed until pain disappears.'],['Diagnose isolated renal salt wasting','The stool findings and pancreatic history favor digestive dysfunction.']], 'Chronic pancreatitis can impair exocrine digestive function and cause steatorrhea, weight loss and nutritional deficits. Pain management alone is incomplete; dietetic assessment and an individualized plan for digestive and nutritional support should address this separate burden.','Assess malabsorption and nutritional risk in chronic pancreatitis with steatorrhea.','pancreatic enzyme physiology'),
q('chronic-pancreatitis-new-diabetes','Pancreatogenic diabetes recognition','Medicine','Endocrine',3,'pancreas','A patient with longstanding chronic pancreatitis and exocrine insufficiency develops persistent hyperglycemia. Which additional endocrine consequence should be considered?',[
['Diabetes secondary to pancreatic disease','Chronic pancreatic damage can impair endocrine as well as exocrine function.'],['Pancreatitis guarantees preserved glucose regulation','Pancreatic injury can affect hormone production and glucose control.'],['Hyperglycemia proves there is no pancreatic disease','Both endocrine and exocrine dysfunction may coexist.'],['Every case must be autoimmune type 1 diabetes','The pancreatic history supplies an alternative mechanism requiring assessment.']], 'Chronic pancreatitis damages pancreatic tissue and can produce diabetes related to pancreatic disease. Recognizing the context matters because digestive insufficiency and altered hormone reserve may coexist, making simple assumptions about standard type 1 or type 2 categories incomplete.','Recognize diabetes as a possible endocrine complication of chronic pancreatitis.','pancreatic endocrine physiology'),
q('cirrhosis-normal-enzymes-not-exclusion','Limits of aminotransferases in cirrhosis','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'cirrhosis','A patient with longstanding liver risk factors has thrombocytopenia, splenomegaly and imaging suggesting portal hypertension, but ALT is normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',[
['Normal aminotransferases do not exclude cirrhosis','Enzyme values alone cannot rule out advanced chronic liver disease.'],['Normal ALT proves the liver is structurally normal','Aminotransferases do not directly measure fibrosis or portal pressure.'],['Splenomegaly becomes irrelevant if ALT is normal','The other clinical and imaging evidence still requires evaluation.'],['Cirrhosis requires massive enzyme elevation in every case','Advanced fibrosis may occur without striking enzyme elevation.']], 'Aminotransferases reflect hepatocyte injury and do not reliably quantify fibrosis or portal hypertension. Thus a normal ALT cannot exclude cirrhosis when other evidence suggests chronic liver disease; the whole clinical and structural assessment must be considered.','Avoid excluding cirrhosis solely because aminotransferases are normal.','obstructive jaundice laboratory pattern'),
q('cirrhosis-hcc-surveillance-purpose','Cancer surveillance in cirrhosis','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',2,'cirrhosis','An adult with established cirrhosis feels well and asks why scheduled liver surveillance is recommended despite no new pain or weight loss. What is the main purpose?',[
['Detect hepatocellular carcinoma before symptoms develop','Cirrhosis increases liver cancer risk and surveillance seeks earlier detection.'],['Prove that fibrosis disappears every month','Surveillance is not a direct demonstration of fibrosis reversal.'],['Replace all assessment of acute decompensation','New symptoms still require assessment outside the surveillance schedule.'],['Screen only after a large mass becomes palpable','Waiting for symptoms can miss the opportunity for earlier diagnosis.']], 'Cirrhosis confers an increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma even when a patient feels well. Surveillance aims to identify cancer before symptomatic presentation; it complements rather than replaces clinical assessment for new decompensation or other liver problems.','Explain the rationale for hepatocellular carcinoma surveillance in cirrhosis.','hepatic pathology'),
q('cirrhosis-varices-screening-purpose','Portal hypertensive complication assessment','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'cirrhosis','A patient with newly recognized cirrhosis asks why assessment for esophageal varices may be needed even though he has never vomited blood. Which explanation is best?',[
['Varices can exist before a first bleeding episode','Portal hypertension may form varices that are initially asymptomatic.'],['Varices occur only after hematemesis causes them','Bleeding is a complication rather than the cause of variceal formation.'],['Absence of pain excludes varices','Varices may be silent before hemorrhage.'],['Assessment of varices confirms every cause of cirrhosis','It evaluates a complication rather than establishing all etiologies.']], 'Portal hypertension can create esophageal varices before any bleeding occurs. Risk assessment therefore concerns prevention of a first hemorrhage as well as management after bleeding; the absence of prior hematemesis does not establish that varices are absent.','Explain why variceal risk may need assessment before first bleeding.','upper gastrointestinal bleeding appearance'),
q('cirrhosis-ascites-infection-assessment','New illness in cirrhotic ascites','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'cirrhosis','A patient with cirrhotic ascites develops fever, abdominal discomfort and worsening renal function. What complication should be urgently evaluated rather than assuming uncomplicated fluid accumulation?',[
['Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis','Ascitic infection can precipitate systemic illness and renal deterioration.'],['Only benign abdominal wall stretching','Fever and renal decline require assessment for infection and decompensation.'],['A guaranteed noninfectious medication side effect','The infectious pattern cannot be dismissed without evaluation.'],['Normal variation requiring no review','These acute changes in cirrhosis are clinically important.']], 'Ascitic infection can produce fever, discomfort and worsening kidney function, sometimes without dramatic abdominal signs. This deterioration requires urgent evaluation for spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and other precipitants rather than treatment as uncomplicated chronic ascites.','Evaluate ascitic infection when cirrhosis decompensates with fever and renal decline.','hepatic encephalopathy'),
q('cirrhosis-confusion-broad-differential','Confusion in cirrhosis without anchoring','Medicine','Nervous System',4,'cirrhosis','A patient with cirrhosis becomes confused and has a glucose of 38 mg/dL. A trainee suggests waiting for an ammonia result before acting. What should be prioritized?',[
['Immediate treatment of hypoglycemia while evaluating other causes','A reversible dangerous metabolic abnormality must be corrected promptly.'],['Assume every confused cirrhotic patient has only hepatic encephalopathy','Cirrhosis does not exclude other causes of altered consciousness.'],['Wait for ammonia before treating any abnormality','Delay would leave documented hypoglycemia untreated.'],['Use ammonia alone to determine all neurological care','A single test cannot replace assessment of competing causes.']], 'Cirrhosis can predispose to encephalopathy, but it does not make every episode of confusion hepatic. Documented severe hypoglycemia is immediately actionable, so correction and a broader precipitant assessment take priority over waiting for a nonspecific ammonia measurement.','Correct severe hypoglycemia before attributing all confusion to hepatic encephalopathy.','hepatic encephalopathy mechanism'),
q('cirrhosis-decompensation-multiorgan','Recognizing liver decompensation','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'cirrhosis','A patient with compensated cirrhosis develops new ascites, jaundice and confusion. Which description best captures the change in clinical status?',[
['New hepatic decompensation requiring clinical reassessment','Ascites jaundice and encephalopathy can mark worsening liver function.'],['Stable compensated disease by definition','New complications indicate a clinically important change.'],['Normal aging independent of liver disease','This combination is not an expected effect of normal aging.'],['Only isolated skin pigmentation','The systemic and neurological findings extend beyond a skin change.']], 'New ascites, jaundice and confusion indicate a major change from compensated cirrhosis and may reflect decompensation. Assessment should identify precipitating illness and complications rather than treating the previous compensated label as a permanent description of status.','Recognize new ascites jaundice and confusion as possible cirrhotic decompensation.','hepatic encephalopathy'),
q('coeliac-autoimmune-risk-context','Coeliac testing in autoimmune risk','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'coeliac','An adult with type 1 diabetes develops persistent diarrhea, weight loss and iron deficiency. Which associated disorder should be considered instead of assuming all symptoms arise from diabetes itself?',[
['Coeliac disease','Type 1 diabetes and malabsorptive symptoms justify coeliac assessment.'],['Only short-term dietary indiscretion','Persistent objective nutritional abnormalities warrant a broader workup.'],['Exclude autoimmune digestive disease automatically','Autoimmune conditions can coexist in one patient.'],['Assume insulin directly causes every gastrointestinal symptom','The pattern suggests an additional disease rather than a universal insulin effect.']], 'Type 1 diabetes is associated with coeliac disease, and the new malabsorptive symptoms strengthen the reason to investigate. A known chronic condition should not obscure a second process, particularly when weight loss and iron deficiency provide objective evidence of impaired nutrition.','Consider associated coeliac disease in symptomatic adults with type 1 diabetes.','autoimmunity'),
q('pancreatitis-pseudocyst-symptomatic','Symptomatic pancreatic fluid collection','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'pancreas','Weeks after pancreatitis, a patient has a pancreatic pseudocyst with persistent vomiting and weight loss from gastric compression. What should be considered with the specialist team?',[
['Assessment for drainage of the symptomatic collection','Compression and nutritional consequences can justify intervention.'],['Assume all pseudocysts are harmless regardless of symptoms','Symptoms and complications affect the need for treatment.'],['Drain every tiny asymptomatic collection immediately','Intervention should reflect symptoms anatomy and complication risk.'],['Only wait for lipase normalization before reassessment','Current compressive symptoms require their own assessment.']], 'A pseudocyst is not treated solely according to its existence. Persistent vomiting and weight loss from compression make this collection clinically consequential, supporting specialist evaluation for appropriate drainage after considering anatomy and procedural options.','Identify compressive symptoms as a reason to assess pseudocyst drainage.','acute pancreatitis diagnostic criteria'),
q('ibs-diet-restriction-dietitian','Restrictive diets in refractory IBS','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'ibs','A patient with IBS remains symptomatic after general dietary advice and wants to eliminate many food groups indefinitely. What is the most appropriate approach to a more restrictive diet?',[
['Use a trained professional to guide a structured dietary trial','Specialist guidance can balance symptom goals with nutritional adequacy.'],['Encourage indefinite unsupervised elimination of all suspected foods','Broad restriction can create nutritional problems without a clear plan.'],['Assume every commercial intolerance test is diagnostic','Unvalidated testing should not replace clinical dietary assessment.'],['Decline all nutrition discussion','Diet can be relevant when advice is individualized and appropriately supported.']], 'More restrictive IBS diets should be considered as structured interventions with appropriate dietary expertise. Professional guidance helps preserve nutrition, define the trial and reassess response rather than allowing progressively broader permanent exclusions without evidence of benefit.','Seek dietary expertise for restrictive IBS dietary interventions.','nutritional assessment')
);
references.asthma=['BTS NICE SIGN NG245: Asthma diagnosis and management','https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng245/chapter/recommendations'];
specs.push(
q('copd-inhaler-technique-before-escalation','Persistent COPD symptoms','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'copd','A patient remains breathless despite prescribed COPD inhalers. During review, he cannot demonstrate correct device use and often misses doses. What should be addressed before routine escalation?',[
['Inhaler technique adherence and device suitability','Poor delivery can mimic treatment failure despite appropriate prescriptions.'],['Add another inhaled drug without observing technique','Additional prescriptions do not correct failure to deliver existing treatment.'],['Start long-term oxygen based only on breathlessness','Oxygen eligibility requires more than a symptom report.'],['Arrange lung surgery as the immediate next step','Basic treatment delivery should be reviewed before advanced intervention.']], 'A prescribed inhaler has little benefit if the patient cannot use it correctly or does not take it. Observing technique, reviewing adherence and matching the device to the patient can resolve apparent nonresponse and should precede reflex treatment escalation.','Correct treatment delivery before escalating apparently ineffective COPD inhalers.','COPD spirometry interpretation'),
q('copd-rehabilitation-functional-disability','Pulmonary rehabilitation referral','Medicine','Respiratory',3,'copd','A stable patient with COPD remains functionally limited by breathlessness despite appropriate medical treatment. He wants to improve walking tolerance and daily activity. Which intervention should be offered?',[
['Pulmonary rehabilitation','A structured program can improve exercise capacity and function in COPD.'],['Bed rest as long-term maintenance','Persistent inactivity promotes deconditioning rather than functional recovery.'],['Antibiotics without an exacerbation','Stable functional limitation does not itself establish bacterial infection.'],['Oxygen solely to improve a normal saturation','Long-term oxygen is not prescribed on exercise preference alone.']], 'Pulmonary rehabilitation combines exercise and education to address COPD-related disability. It complements appropriate medicines and targets deconditioning and functional limitations, whereas unsupported antibiotics or routine inactivity do not address the stable activity goal.','Offer pulmonary rehabilitation for functionally limiting stable COPD.','COPD spirometry interpretation'),
q('copd-hemoptysis-alternative-diagnosis','Atypical symptoms in COPD','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'copd','A patient with COPD develops persistent hemoptysis, unexplained weight loss and a change in cough. What is the most appropriate next approach?',[
['Investigate another cause including lung malignancy','Hemoptysis and weight loss should not be attributed automatically to COPD.'],['Increase bronchodilators as the only action','Bronchodilation does not evaluate cancer or another bleeding source.'],['Assume stable chronic bronchitis explains every change','A new concerning pattern requires reassessment beyond the existing label.'],['Repeat spirometry alone and stop assessment if unchanged','Unchanged obstruction cannot exclude a separate pulmonary lesion.']], 'COPD can coexist with lung malignancy and other causes of hemoptysis. New weight loss and a changing cough are warning features that warrant further investigation; measuring familiar obstruction alone would not address the new differential.','Investigate hemoptysis and weight loss rather than anchoring on COPD.','COPD spirometry interpretation'),
q('copd-niv-persistent-acidosis','Ventilatory support during COPD exacerbation','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'copd','A cooperative patient with a COPD exacerbation remains hypercapnic and acidotic despite appropriate initial medical therapy and controlled oxygen. He can protect his airway. Which escalation should be considered?',[
['Noninvasive ventilation with appropriate monitoring','Persistent hypercapnic ventilatory failure can benefit from NIV in a suitable patient.'],['Unmonitored discharge with the same regimen','Ongoing respiratory acidosis indicates unresolved ventilatory failure.'],['Long-term oxygen assessment as the only acute action','An outpatient oxygen pathway does not treat current ventilatory failure.'],['Increase oxygen without reassessing ventilation','More oxygen alone does not correct impaired alveolar ventilation.']], 'Persistent hypercapnic respiratory acidosis despite initial treatment indicates inadequate ventilation. In a patient able to cooperate and protect the airway, monitored noninvasive ventilation should be considered, with continued reassessment for treatment failure and need for invasive support.','Consider NIV for persistent acidotic hypercapnic failure after initial COPD treatment.','respiratory acidosis physiology'),
q('copd-ltot-stable-assessment','Long-term oxygen eligibility assessment','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'copd','A patient had low oxygen saturation during a COPD exacerbation but is improving. He asks whether that single acute reading proves he needs permanent home oxygen. What is the best response?',[
['Reassess eligibility when stable using the appropriate oxygen pathway','Acute hypoxemia alone does not establish chronic oxygen requirements.'],['Prescribe lifelong oxygen from one acute reading','Exacerbation-associated hypoxemia may improve with recovery.'],['Use breathlessness severity alone as the qualifying test','Symptoms do not replace objective assessment of chronic hypoxemia.'],['Exclude oxygen forever because he is improving','Improvement does not prove chronic eligibility criteria will never be met.']], 'Long-term oxygen decisions require an assessment of persistent hypoxemia under appropriate stable conditions. A low reading during an exacerbation may reflect reversible acute illness, so it should prompt follow-up rather than automatic permanent treatment or permanent exclusion.','Separate acute exacerbation hypoxemia from stable long-term oxygen eligibility.','COPD gas exchange'),
q('copd-still-smoking-cessation-support','Smoking cessation in COPD','Medicine','Respiratory',2,'copd','A patient with established COPD continues to smoke and believes quitting is pointless because lung damage has already occurred. Which counseling approach is best?',[
['Offer practical smoking-cessation support despite established disease','Stopping smoking remains valuable even after COPD has developed.'],['Agree that stopping cannot benefit established COPD','Existing damage does not eliminate the value of reducing further exposure.'],['Discuss only inhaler brands and omit smoking','Medication review should not displace an important modifiable risk factor.'],['Wait until a transplant assessment before offering support','Cessation support is relevant earlier in the disease course.']], 'Smoking cessation remains an important intervention after COPD is established because continued exposure contributes to further harm. Counseling should include practical treatment and support rather than presenting cessation as useful only before disease begins.','Offer smoking-cessation support throughout established COPD care.','emphysema pathogenesis'),
q('pneumonia-crb65-integration','Pneumonia severity assessment','Medicine','Respiratory',3,'pneumonia','A 70-year-old with community-acquired pneumonia is confused, has respiratory rate 32/min and blood pressure 86/54 mm Hg. Which interpretation best guides place-of-care assessment?',[
['Several CRB65 severity features support urgent hospital assessment','Confusion tachypnea hypotension and age all increase severity concern.'],['Normal oxygenation alone would guarantee outpatient safety','A single reassuring parameter does not negate the other severe features.'],['Age is the only clinically relevant feature','Physiological abnormalities and confusion independently matter.'],['A severity score replaces clinical judgment completely','Scores support but do not replace overall assessment and circumstances.']], 'This patient has multiple CRB65 severity features, including confusion, rapid breathing, low blood pressure and age over 65. These findings support urgent hospital-level assessment, while the score remains an aid to clinical judgment rather than an autonomous decision maker.','Integrate multiple pneumonia severity features into urgent place-of-care assessment.','pneumonia respiratory physiology'),
q('pneumonia-score-clinical-context','Severity score limitations in pneumonia','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'pneumonia','A younger adult with pneumonia has a low CRB65 score but is severely hypoxemic and unable to keep down oral medication. How should the low score affect disposition?',[
['It should not override the need for hospital-level care','Hypoxemia and inability to take treatment are important additional constraints.'],['It mandates home treatment regardless of other findings','A score cannot negate dangerous clinical features outside its components.'],['It proves the infection is viral','A mortality score does not establish microbiological cause.'],['It eliminates the need to assess social support','Practical treatment safety still influences care setting.']], 'CRB65 does not capture every determinant of safe care. Significant hypoxemia and inability to take oral treatment can require hospital management despite a low numerical score, so clinical judgment and individual constraints must accompany score-based risk assessment.','Override a low pneumonia score when major clinical constraints make home care unsafe.','severity-score interpretation'),
q('pneumonia-followup-risk-based-imaging','Selective chest imaging after pneumonia','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'pneumonia','After pneumonia treatment, a 63-year-old smoker still has persistent cough and unexplained weight loss. What follow-up should be considered rather than assuming complete resolution?',[
['Follow-up chest imaging to assess persistent disease or malignancy','Persistent symptoms and cancer risk support further imaging assessment.'],['No review because antibiotics were completed','Completion of treatment does not prove resolution or exclude an underlying lesion.'],['Routine annual spirometry as the only follow-up','Spirometry does not address a persistent focal opacity or occult malignancy.'],['Indefinite empiric antibiotics without reassessment','Repeated treatment can delay identification of the underlying cause.']], 'Persistent symptoms after pneumonia, especially in a person at higher risk of lung cancer, support consideration of follow-up chest imaging. The purpose is to assess resolution and possible underlying disease rather than equating completion of an antibiotic course with diagnostic closure.','Consider follow-up imaging after pneumonia when symptoms and malignancy risk persist.','lobar consolidation'),
q('pneumonia-recovery-safety-net','Expected recovery and deterioration advice','Medicine','Respiratory',3,'pneumonia','A patient starting community treatment for pneumonia asks whether any worsening should be ignored until the antibiotic course ends. Which advice is most appropriate?',[
['Seek reassessment if symptoms worsen or fail to improve as expected','Recovery advice should include clear deterioration and nonresponse triggers.'],['Wait until the course ends despite new severe breathlessness','Acute deterioration can require earlier reassessment.'],['Stop treatment whenever fever first falls','Early symptom improvement does not define the planned treatment duration.'],['Assume fatigue proves antibiotic allergy','Fatigue can occur during recovery and is not specific for allergy.']], 'Patients should understand the expected course and the signs that warrant reassessment. Worsening breathlessness, systemic illness or failure to improve should not be ignored merely because an antibiotic course remains unfinished; follow-up depends on the evolving clinical picture.','Provide deterioration and nonresponse advice during community pneumonia treatment.','pneumonia clinical assessment'),
q('asthma-confirm-objective-evidence','Objective diagnosis of adult asthma','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'asthma','A clinically stable adult has episodic wheeze and cough but no previous objective testing. Which approach best establishes a robust asthma diagnosis?',[
['Combine the compatible history with appropriate objective tests','Asthma diagnosis should be supported by physiological or inflammatory evidence.'],['Diagnose solely from the word wheeze','Wheeze has alternative causes and history alone can misclassify disease.'],['Require a chest CT in every uncomplicated case','Routine CT is not the usual first objective confirmation pathway.'],['Use response to a single cough lozenge as confirmation','A nonspecific symptomatic response cannot establish asthma.']], 'Asthma assessment combines a suggestive clinical history with suitable objective evidence, such as inflammatory markers or variable airflow obstruction within the recommended pathway. This reduces misclassification because episodic cough and wheeze can also arise from other conditions.','Support an adult asthma history with appropriate objective testing.','airway obstruction physiology'),
q('asthma-tests-negative-persistent-suspicion','Unconfirmed suspected asthma','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'asthma','An adult has a strongly suggestive asthma history, but initial recommended objective tests have not confirmed it. Symptoms persist and alternatives have been reviewed. What is an appropriate next diagnostic step?',[
['Specialist consideration of a bronchial challenge test','Further testing may clarify asthma when initial objective tests are nondiagnostic.'],['Exclude asthma forever from one nondiagnostic result','Initial tests may not establish every intermittent case.'],['Declare COPD solely because asthma tests are inconclusive','A competing diagnosis requires its own evidence.'],['Continue escalating unconfirmed treatment indefinitely','Persistent diagnostic uncertainty warrants review rather than automatic escalation.']], 'Intermittent asthma can remain unconfirmed after initial testing. If clinical suspicion persists despite the recommended sequence and alternative diagnoses are considered, referral for bronchial challenge testing can help clarify variable airway responsiveness rather than forcing an unsupported label.','Consider bronchial challenge after nondiagnostic initial asthma testing with persistent suspicion.','airway reactivity'),
q('asthma-control-multidomain','Assessing asthma control beyond daytime symptoms','Medicine','Respiratory',3,'asthma','A patient reports little daytime wheeze but wakes with asthma twice weekly and avoids exercise because of symptoms. How should control be interpreted?',[
['Control is incomplete despite few daytime symptoms','Night waking and activity limitation are important dimensions of control.'],['Asthma is fully controlled if daytime wheeze is rare','This ignores nocturnal symptoms and exercise limitation.'],['Exercise avoidance proves no current disease burden','Avoidance can conceal rather than remove activity-related symptoms.'],['Only a chest radiograph can measure symptom control','Control assessment includes clinical symptoms and functional effects.']], 'Asthma control includes nighttime symptoms, activity limitation, reliever need and attacks as well as daytime symptoms. A patient may report little wheeze because activities are restricted, so these additional domains must be assessed before declaring good control.','Assess nighttime symptoms and activity restriction when judging asthma control.','airway obstruction physiology'),
q('af-irregular-pulse-ecg-confirmation','Confirming suspected atrial fibrillation','Medicine','Cardiovascular',2,'af','A patient has an irregular pulse and intermittent palpitations. What investigation should be used to confirm suspected atrial fibrillation when the rhythm is present?',[
['A 12-lead ECG','An ECG documents rhythm characteristics needed to confirm AF.'],['A chest radiograph alone','A radiograph does not establish the electrical rhythm.'],['An elevated D-dimer alone','D-dimer reflects fibrin turnover rather than atrial activation.'],['Echocardiography alone without rhythm recording','Echo informs structure but does not replace ECG confirmation.']], 'An irregular pulse raises suspicion for atrial fibrillation, but rhythm documentation is needed. A 12-lead ECG can demonstrate the characteristic rhythm and guide further evaluation; structural or blood tests provide different information and cannot substitute for it.','Confirm suspected atrial fibrillation with ECG rhythm documentation.','cardiac conduction physiology'),
q('paroxysmal-af-ambulatory-recording','Intermittent palpitations with normal ECG','Medicine','Cardiovascular',4,'af','A patient has recurrent brief palpitations, but the clinic ECG is normal between episodes. Paroxysmal AF remains suspected. What is the most useful next strategy?',[
['Ambulatory rhythm monitoring matched to episode frequency','Intermittent recording can capture a rhythm absent during clinic assessment.'],['Exclude AF permanently from the normal clinic ECG','An interepisode ECG may miss paroxysmal atrial fibrillation.'],['Use a single chest radiograph to confirm AF','Imaging cannot document an intermittent electrical rhythm.'],['Start an antiarrhythmic solely from symptom description','The suspected rhythm should be documented where possible.']], 'Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation may be absent during a short clinic recording. Ambulatory monitoring selected for the event frequency improves the chance of capturing an episode and avoids treating a normal interepisode ECG as a definitive exclusion.','Use suitable ambulatory monitoring for suspected paroxysmal AF with a normal clinic ECG.','cardiac conduction physiology'),
q('af-hemodynamic-instability-cardioversion','Unstable atrial fibrillation','Medicine','Cardiovascular',4,'af','A patient with rapid AF develops shock and ongoing ischemic chest pain attributed to the rhythm. What intervention takes priority in this life-threatening unstable presentation?',[
['Emergency electrical cardioversion','Immediate rhythm correction is indicated when AF causes dangerous instability.'],['Delay all action for a routine outpatient echocardiogram','Life-threatening instability requires treatment before routine outpatient testing.'],['Use aspirin alone to treat the acute rhythm','An antiplatelet does not correct the unstable tachyarrhythmia.'],['Wait several weeks for spontaneous resolution','Shock and ischemia make passive observation inappropriate.']], 'When atrial fibrillation is causing life-threatening hemodynamic instability, emergency electrical cardioversion takes priority. Stroke-prevention assessment and investigation remain important, but should not delay stabilization of shock and ongoing ischemia attributed to the arrhythmia.','Prioritize emergency cardioversion for AF causing life-threatening instability.','cardiac arrhythmia physiology'),
q('af-aspirin-not-anticoagulation-substitute','Stroke prevention in AF','Medicine','Cardiovascular',4,'af','An adult with AF has a clear indication for oral anticoagulation after stroke and bleeding risk review. He asks whether aspirin alone gives equivalent protection for this purpose. What is the best response?',[
['Aspirin alone is not an equivalent AF stroke-prevention substitute','Appropriate oral anticoagulation is the relevant stroke-prevention strategy.'],['Aspirin is always equivalent regardless of stroke risk','Antiplatelet therapy alone is not equivalent for AF-related embolic prevention.'],['Rate control by itself removes all embolic risk','Controlling ventricular rate does not eliminate thromboembolic risk.'],['A normal pulse today permanently removes the indication','A single rhythm observation does not replace ongoing risk assessment.']], 'Atrial fibrillation-related stroke prevention is based on thromboembolic risk and the suitability of anticoagulation. Aspirin monotherapy is not an equivalent substitute when anticoagulation is indicated, and satisfactory rate control does not independently remove embolic risk.','Distinguish oral anticoagulation from aspirin alone for indicated AF stroke prevention.','platelet versus coagulation pharmacology'),
q('af-falls-age-anticoagulation-review','Anticoagulation in older adults with AF','Geriatrics','Cardiovascular',4,'af','An older adult with AF and substantial stroke risk has had one mechanical fall. There is no active bleeding. Should age or fall risk alone automatically exclude anticoagulation?',[
['No assess individual stroke bleeding and modifiable risks','Guidance advises against withholding solely because of age or fall risk.'],['Yes any past fall is an absolute contraindication','A fall history alone does not establish an absolute prohibition.'],['Yes age always eliminates treatment benefit','Older age can also increase the underlying embolic risk.'],['No bleeding assessment is needed in older adults','A balanced individualized assessment still remains essential.']], 'Age and falls must be considered within an individualized risk discussion, but neither alone automatically justifies withholding anticoagulation. Assessment should weigh stroke prevention against bleeding risk and address modifiable contributors rather than applying a blanket age-based exclusion.','Avoid withholding AF anticoagulation solely on age or fall risk.','medical ethics older adult treatment'),
q('af-bleeding-risk-modifiable','Bleeding risk review before anticoagulation','Medicine','Cardiovascular',4,'af','A patient with AF needs stroke-prevention assessment and has uncontrolled blood pressure, hazardous alcohol use and intermittent NSAID use. How should these findings be used?',[
['Identify and address modifiable bleeding risks during the decision','Risk review should guide correction of factors that can increase harm.'],['Ignore them once a stroke score is calculated','Stroke risk does not replace evaluation of preventable bleeding hazards.'],['Treat every bleeding risk factor as an absolute permanent exclusion','Several risk factors can be modified rather than used as a blanket prohibition.'],['Use a bleeding score to select the exact arrhythmia type','Bleeding assessment does not classify the cardiac rhythm.']], 'Bleeding risk assessment is useful partly because it identifies modifiable factors, including uncontrolled hypertension, alcohol exposure and interacting medicines. These should inform a balanced treatment discussion and active risk reduction, rather than automatic exclusion or disregard of preventable harms.','Address modifiable bleeding hazards when considering AF anticoagulation.','drug and clinical bleeding risk'),
q('hypertension-abpm-confirm','Confirming clinic hypertension','Medicine','Cardiovascular',4,'bp','A patient has repeated clinic blood pressures around 152/94 mm Hg without symptoms of an emergency. What generally helps confirm hypertension before committing to long-term treatment?',[
['Ambulatory or appropriately performed home blood-pressure monitoring','Out-of-office measurements help establish sustained rather than clinic-only elevation.'],['Diagnose from one hurried reading alone','A single poorly standardized measurement can misclassify pressure.'],['Use an ECG alone as the confirmatory pressure test','ECG may assess consequences but does not measure sustained blood pressure.'],['Assume no symptoms excludes hypertension','Hypertension is often asymptomatic despite important risk.']], 'Repeated clinic elevation should usually be assessed with ambulatory or suitable home measurements when there is no emergency. This helps distinguish sustained hypertension from a clinic-related effect and supports a reliable long-term diagnosis rather than reliance on one context.','Confirm nonemergency clinic hypertension using appropriate out-of-office measurements.','blood pressure physiology'),
q('white-coat-hypertension-discordance','Clinic and ambulatory pressure discordance','Medicine','Cardiovascular',3,'bp','A patient has high office pressures but consistently normal well-performed ambulatory readings. Which pattern does this suggest?',[
['A white-coat effect or white-coat hypertension pattern','Pressure elevation confined to the clinic differs from sustained hypertension.'],['Masked hypertension','Masked hypertension has higher out-of-office than clinic pressure.'],['Guaranteed hypertensive emergency','A setting-related discrepancy does not establish acute target-organ injury.'],['Measurement concordance','The two settings show a discrepancy rather than agreement.']], 'Higher clinic pressure with normal out-of-office readings suggests a white-coat pattern. This differs from masked hypertension, where routine clinic measurements underrepresent pressure outside the clinic, and requires contextual follow-up rather than an assumption of identical risk in all settings.','Identify the direction of clinic-versus-ambulatory discrepancy in a white-coat pattern.','blood pressure measurement'),
q('masked-hypertension-outofoffice','Masked hypertension pattern','Medicine','Cardiovascular',3,'bp','An adult has normal clinic pressure but repeatedly elevated validated home readings. What pattern should be considered rather than dismissing the home measurements?',[
['Masked hypertension','Out-of-office hypertension may be missed by normal clinic measurements.'],['White-coat hypertension','A white-coat pattern has the opposite direction of discrepancy.'],['Normal pressure established in every setting','A normal clinic value does not invalidate consistent appropriate home readings.'],['Hypertensive emergency solely from discordance','Emergency status requires assessment for severe acute injury rather than discrepancy alone.']], 'Masked hypertension describes elevated pressure outside the clinic despite normal clinic readings. Reliable home or ambulatory evidence therefore deserves assessment; selecting only the reassuring setting can miss a clinically important sustained exposure.','Recognize elevated out-of-office pressure despite normal clinic measurements.','blood pressure measurement'),
q('bp-irregular-pulse-manual','Blood-pressure technique with irregular pulse','Medicine','Cardiovascular',3,'bp','A patient has an irregular pulse suggestive of AF. An automated cuff gives highly variable readings. Which adjustment improves the blood-pressure assessment?',[
['Use an appropriate manual auscultatory measurement','Pulse irregularity can reduce the accuracy of some automated devices.'],['Accept only the lowest automated result','Selecting the lowest value introduces bias rather than addressing device limitations.'],['Use pulse rate as a substitute for pressure','Heart rate does not directly provide systolic and diastolic pressure.'],['Measure only calf circumference','A limb circumference does not assess systemic arterial pressure.']], 'An irregular pulse can interfere with automated blood-pressure measurement. Appropriate manual auscultatory measurement addresses that limitation; averaging or selecting favorable automated readings without checking technique can misrepresent the true pressure.','Use suitable manual blood-pressure measurement when pulse irregularity impairs automated readings.','cardiac conduction physiology'),
q('bp-both-arms-initial','Interarm pressure difference','Medicine','Cardiovascular',4,'bp','At an initial hypertension assessment, one arm repeatedly reads substantially higher than the other after correct positioning. What should guide subsequent routine measurement?',[
['Use the higher-reading arm after confirming the persistent difference','This avoids systematically underestimating pressure from the lower arm.'],['Always use the lower-reading arm','That choice can underestimate the clinically relevant pressure.'],['Average the arm circumference instead of pressures','Circumference does not resolve the interarm pressure difference.'],['Treat the lower value as proof that hypertension is impossible','A reproducible higher-arm pressure remains important.']], 'Initial assessment should consider both arms and confirm a substantial difference. Using the higher-reading arm for subsequent measurements avoids systematic underestimation, while the difference itself may also merit clinical interpretation according to the broader context.','Use the higher-reading arm after confirming a persistent interarm pressure difference.','blood pressure measurement'),
q('resistant-hypertension-check-adherence','Apparent resistant hypertension','Medicine','Cardiovascular',4,'bp','A patient has high clinic pressure despite three prescribed antihypertensives. Out-of-office pressure, actual adherence and postural symptoms have not been reviewed. What should be done before labeling true resistant hypertension?',[
['Confirm pressure and review adherence and treatment factors','Apparent resistance can reflect clinic effect or inadequate actual treatment exposure.'],['Add several drugs immediately without review','Escalation can cause harm if apparent resistance reflects measurement or adherence issues.'],['Assume every prescription is taken correctly','A prescription list does not establish actual adherence.'],['Exclude all secondary causes permanently','Persistent confirmed resistance may warrant further secondary-cause assessment.']], 'Apparent resistant hypertension requires verification of sustained pressure and the treatment actually taken. Out-of-office measurements, adherence and medication review can reveal pseudoresistance and prevent inappropriate escalation, while confirmed resistance can prompt a further targeted evaluation.','Check sustained pressure and actual treatment exposure before labeling resistant hypertension.','primary hyperaldosteronism mechanism')
);
specs.push(
q('ra-persistent-synovitis-negative-rf','Seronegative inflammatory arthritis referral','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',4,'ra','An adult has persistent swelling of several small hand joints and prolonged morning stiffness. Rheumatoid factor is negative and CRP is normal. What should happen next?',[
['Refer for specialist assessment of persistent synovitis','Normal markers or negative serology do not exclude early inflammatory arthritis.'],['Exclude rheumatoid arthritis solely from negative rheumatoid factor','Seronegativity does not eliminate a clinically inflammatory presentation.'],['Wait for radiographic erosions before referral','Delaying until structural damage appears can miss an early treatment opportunity.'],['Treat only with an analgesic and never reassess','Symptom relief does not address persistent inflammatory joint disease.']], 'Persistent small-joint synovitis warrants specialist assessment even with negative rheumatoid factor and normal inflammatory markers. Early inflammatory arthritis can be seronegative, and waiting for erosions risks avoidable damage rather than improving diagnostic certainty safely.','Refer persistent small-joint synovitis despite negative rheumatoid factor and normal markers.','autoimmune joint pathology'),
q('ra-nsaids-not-disease-modifying','RA symptom relief versus damage prevention','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',3,'ra','A patient with active rheumatoid arthritis feels less pain after an NSAID but still has swollen joints. What is the main limitation of relying on that response alone?',[
['Pain relief does not establish control of destructive synovitis','NSAIDs can reduce symptoms without replacing disease-modifying treatment.'],['Any pain response proves permanent remission','Less pain does not demonstrate absence of active disease.'],['An NSAID response excludes rheumatoid arthritis','Inflammatory pain can improve symptomatically without altering diagnosis.'],['Joint swelling becomes irrelevant when pain improves','Persistent swelling remains evidence requiring disease-activity assessment.']], 'Analgesic or anti-inflammatory symptom relief is distinct from controlling the disease process. Persistent synovitis despite less pain means RA activity still needs assessment and disease-modifying management, because comfort alone does not demonstrate prevention of structural damage.','Distinguish symptomatic NSAID benefit from disease control in RA.','inflammatory joint pathology'),
q('ra-treat-target-remission','RA treatment target','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',2,'ra','A patient begins a disease-modifying treatment plan for active rheumatoid arthritis. Which goal best captures the usual target of ongoing disease-activity assessment?',[
['Remission or low disease activity when remission is not achievable','A defined activity target guides monitoring and adjustment.'],['Pain relief alone regardless of joint inflammation','Pain is important but does not fully represent inflammatory control.'],['A positive rheumatoid factor on every visit','Seropositivity is not the therapeutic goal.'],['A fixed unreviewed prescription despite persistent swelling','Treatment should respond to measured disease activity.']], 'RA treatment uses an explicit target, generally remission or low activity when remission cannot be achieved. Serial clinical assessment informs adjustment toward that goal, rather than equating a stable prescription or isolated laboratory feature with satisfactory control.','Identify remission or low disease activity as the RA treat-to-target goal.','autoimmune inflammation'),
q('ra-annual-comorbidity-review','RA care beyond swollen joints','Medicine','General Principles',3,'ra','A patient with RA reaches low disease activity and asks whether annual review can now focus only on joints. Which additional review remains appropriate?',[
['Cardiovascular bone health mood and functional comorbidities','RA care includes important associated health and functional burdens.'],['No review until severe synovitis returns','Controlled inflammation does not eliminate comorbidity or disability risks.'],['Rheumatoid factor alone as the entire review','Serology cannot assess cardiovascular risk osteoporosis or functioning.'],['Only a pain score with no history','A narrow score misses clinically important consequences outside joints.']], 'An annual RA review assesses more than current joint swelling. Function, cardiovascular risk, osteoporosis, mood and possible extra-articular complications remain relevant even when the inflammatory target is met, so stable disease does not remove the need for broader care.','Include comorbidity and function assessment in an annual RA review.','chronic inflammatory disease'),
q('ra-cervical-neurological-redflags','Cervical complications in RA','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',4,'ra','A patient with longstanding RA develops neck pain, gait disturbance and new hand clumsiness. Which complication should be urgently considered?',[
['Cervical spine disease with cord compromise','RA can affect the cervical spine and produce neurological complications.'],['Simple wrist synovitis as the sole explanation','A wrist problem does not explain gait disturbance with neck symptoms.'],['Only deconditioning from reduced activity','New focal neurological changes require structural assessment.'],['An uncomplicated medication-related rash','A skin reaction does not explain the motor pattern.']], 'RA can involve the cervical spine, and new neck symptoms with gait or hand dysfunction raise concern for neurological compromise. These findings warrant urgent specialist assessment rather than attributing them entirely to peripheral joint pain or inactivity.','Escalate neck pain with neurological symptoms in longstanding RA.','cervical cord dysfunction'),
q('ra-respiratory-complication-assessment','New respiratory symptoms in RA','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'ra','A patient with RA reports new progressive dry cough and exertional dyspnea. Joint symptoms are controlled. What is the best next approach?',[
['Assess pulmonary complications treatment effects and other causes','Controlled joints do not exclude lung disease or medication-related problems.'],['Assume controlled joints rule out all RA-related complications','Extra-articular disease can require separate evaluation.'],['Escalate a cough suppressant without respiratory assessment','New progressive dyspnea needs etiological evaluation.'],['Measure rheumatoid factor only and stop if unchanged','A stable antibody value does not exclude pulmonary pathology.']], 'RA care includes assessment for lung complications, and treatment-related respiratory problems may also enter the differential. New progressive dyspnea therefore deserves targeted evaluation despite controlled joint disease, rather than reassurance from a stable arthritis score alone.','Evaluate new progressive respiratory symptoms independently of RA joint control.','extra-articular autoimmune disease'),
q('ra-hand-function-occupational-therapy','Functional support in RA','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',3,'ra','A patient with stable RA struggles to open containers and perform work tasks because of reduced hand function. Which non-drug input should be considered alongside disease management?',[
['Occupational therapy for task adaptation and joint protection','Functional support can reduce practical disability despite stable disease activity.'],['Increase immunosuppression solely from difficulty opening a jar','Functional impairment requires assessment rather than automatic drug escalation.'],['Recommend permanent avoidance of every hand activity','Individualized adaptation is preferable to indiscriminate inactivity.'],['Repeat antibody tests instead of functional assessment','Serology does not identify the practical supports needed for daily tasks.']], 'Daily task limitations may persist even when inflammation is stable. Occupational therapy can assess work and self-care demands, offer adaptations and support joint protection, complementing disease management without assuming every functional difficulty requires increased immunosuppression.','Use occupational therapy for persistent task-related hand disability in RA.','musculoskeletal functional assessment'),
q('ra-foot-problems-podiatry','Foot care in inflammatory arthritis','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',2,'ra','An adult with RA has painful forefoot deformity and difficulty finding shoes despite improved overall disease activity. Which service directly addresses this functional problem?',[
['Podiatry assessment','Foot-specific assessment can address pain footwear and mechanical needs.'],['Audiology alone','Hearing assessment does not address painful forefoot mechanics.'],['Only repeat rheumatoid factor testing','A serology result cannot provide footwear or mechanical support.'],['No further care because inflammation has improved','Residual foot disability remains a legitimate treatment need.']], 'RA can leave persistent foot pain and mechanical disability even when overall disease activity improves. Podiatry assessment targets footwear and foot-specific management, illustrating why inflammatory control and practical rehabilitation are complementary aspects of care.','Refer RA-associated painful foot disability for podiatry assessment.','musculoskeletal biomechanics'),
q('gout-normal-urate-during-flare','Serum urate during acute gout','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',4,'gout','A patient has a strongly suggestive gout flare, but serum urate measured during the attack is below 6 mg/dL. How should this result affect evaluation?',[
['Repeat urate after the flare if suspicion remains high','Serum urate can be below the diagnostic threshold during a flare.'],['Exclude gout permanently from this single acute value','A low acute value does not reliably exclude a compatible flare.'],['Diagnose septic arthritis solely from the urate result','Infection assessment depends on the clinical picture and appropriate testing.'],['Begin indefinite antibiotics based on urate alone','Urate does not identify bacterial joint infection.']], 'Serum urate can be lower during an acute gout flare. When the clinical presentation remains strongly suggestive, repeating it after the flare has settled and using further assessment when needed is more appropriate than permanently excluding gout from one acute measurement.','Recheck urate after an acute flare when gout remains strongly suspected.','purine metabolism'),
q('gout-uncertain-aspiration','Confirming uncertain crystal arthritis','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',4,'gout','An adult has recurrent inflammatory ankle attacks, but the history and serum urate have not established the diagnosis. A joint effusion is accessible. Which test can directly clarify a crystal cause?',[
['Joint aspiration with synovial fluid microscopy','Fluid examination can identify crystals and assist the differential.'],['A repeat nonspecific pain score alone','A severity score does not establish the inflammatory etiology.'],['Assume every recurrent ankle attack is gout','The uncertain presentation warrants objective clarification.'],['Rheumatoid factor alone as definitive proof','That antibody does not directly identify monosodium urate crystals.']], 'When gout remains uncertain, synovial aspiration and microscopy can supply direct evidence of crystals. The result should be interpreted with the full clinical picture, including infection when relevant, rather than substituting a nonspecific pain score or assumption based on recurrence.','Use synovial microscopy to clarify an uncertain crystal arthritis diagnosis.','septic arthritis reasoning'),
q('gout-flare-renal-comorbidity-treatment','Comorbidity-informed gout flare care','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',4,'gout','A patient has a confirmed gout flare, advanced kidney disease and a recent peptic ulcer bleed. Infection has been excluded. What should most influence the choice among first-line anti-inflammatory options?',[
['Comorbidities co-prescriptions and individual treatment risks','NSAID toxicity and alternatives must be weighed against the clinical context.'],['Always choose an NSAID regardless of bleeding and renal risk','The stated comorbidities materially change NSAID safety.'],['Use urate-lowering treatment alone for immediate pain relief','Long-term urate lowering is not a substitute for acute anti-inflammatory care.'],['Base the choice only on the peak pain score','Pain intensity does not replace safety assessment.']], 'Gout flare options include NSAIDs, colchicine or a short oral corticosteroid course, but selection is individualized. Advanced kidney disease and recent ulcer bleeding make a routine NSAID choice problematic, so comorbidity and co-prescription review must guide the acute plan.','Use renal and bleeding risks when selecting acute gout anti-inflammatory treatment.','NSAID renal and gastrointestinal effects'),
q('gout-tophi-urate-lowering-indication','Long-term treatment for recurrent gout','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',4,'gout','An adult has frequent gout flares and visible tophi despite repeated short courses of acute anti-inflammatory treatment. What additional long-term discussion is indicated?',[
['Urate-lowering therapy with a treat-to-target plan','Frequent flares and tophi support long-term reduction of urate burden.'],['Acute anti-inflammatory courses alone indefinitely','Flare treatment does not eliminate the underlying urate crystal burden.'],['Stop follow-up whenever the current pain ends','Recurrent disease and tophi require a preventive management plan.'],['Antibiotics for every painless tophus','Tophi do not themselves establish bacterial infection.']], 'Frequent flares and tophi indicate a substantial urate crystal burden. Acute treatment relieves inflammation but does not address that burden, so long-term urate-lowering therapy and monitoring should be discussed to prevent future attacks and promote crystal dissolution.','Offer a long-term urate-lowering discussion for frequent gout flares with tophi.','purine metabolism'),
q('gout-treat-target-monitoring','Monitoring urate-lowering therapy','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',3,'gout','A patient begins urate-lowering therapy for recurrent gout. He is pain-free today and asks why serum urate will still be checked while the dose is adjusted. What is the best explanation?',[
['The dose is titrated toward a serum urate target','Pain-free intervals do not prove that the urate crystal burden is controlled.'],['Absence of pain proves all crystals disappeared immediately','Symptoms can remit while urate deposits persist.'],['Urate testing confirms whether the joint is infected','Serum urate does not diagnose a bacterial infection.'],['The drug dose can never be adjusted','Treat-to-target care uses response and tolerability to inform titration.']], 'Urate-lowering therapy is adjusted according to serum urate and clinical circumstances rather than the pain level on one day. A pain-free interval does not establish elimination of crystals, so planned monitoring supports effective long-term disease control.','Explain treat-to-target urate monitoring despite a pain-free interval.','purine metabolism'),
q('gout-major-cvd-allopurinol-choice','Urate-lowering choice with cardiovascular disease','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',4,'gout','A patient with recurrent gout and previous myocardial infarction is choosing initial urate-lowering treatment under NICE guidance. Which agent is preferred first line in major cardiovascular disease?',[
['Allopurinol','NICE recommends allopurinol first line when major cardiovascular disease is present.'],['Febuxostat regardless of cardiovascular history','The stated NICE recommendation gives allopurinol priority in this setting.'],['Indefinite high-dose ibuprofen as urate lowering','Ibuprofen relieves inflammation but does not provide urate-lowering treatment.'],['Prednisolone as permanent urate-lowering monotherapy','A glucocorticoid does not reduce the urate crystal burden.']], 'NICE guidance specifically favors allopurinol as first-line urate-lowering therapy in people with major cardiovascular disease. The decision concerns long-term urate reduction, which is distinct from treating an acute flare with an anti-inflammatory medicine.','Select NICE-preferred first-line urate lowering when major cardiovascular disease is present.','purine metabolism pharmacology'),
q('gout-ult-initiation-flare-prophylaxis','Preventing flares during urate lowering','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',3,'gout','A patient starting urate-lowering therapy is warned that dose initiation and titration may provoke flares. Which additional discussion is appropriate?',[
['Consider anti-inflammatory prophylaxis during initiation and titration','Changing urate levels can transiently increase flare risk.'],['Promise that no further flare can occur after the first dose','Early flares can occur despite a sound long-term treatment strategy.'],['Stop urate lowering forever if any early flare occurs','An early flare does not automatically show that preventive treatment is ineffective.'],['Use an antibiotic as routine flare prevention','Gout inflammation is not routinely prevented with antibiotics.']], 'Urate-lowering therapy can transiently provoke flares while urate deposits change. Appropriate anti-inflammatory prophylaxis should be discussed during initiation and titration, and patients need to understand that an early flare does not negate the long-term preventive aim.','Discuss flare prophylaxis when initiating and titrating urate-lowering therapy.','purine metabolism'),
q('gout-cold-therapy-adjunct','Supportive measures during gout flare','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',2,'gout','A patient receiving appropriate medication for a gout flare asks whether a non-drug measure can help relieve joint pain. Which adjunct may be discussed?',[
['Local cold therapy used safely','Cold therapy can complement medication for acute flare pain.'],['Ice as a replacement for every indicated medication','An adjunct does not automatically replace necessary anti-inflammatory care.'],['Forceful exercise through severe acute pain','This is unlikely to be a suitable pain-relief strategy for an inflamed joint.'],['Prolonged direct ice exposure causing skin injury','Cold treatment should be applied with measures that protect the skin.']], 'Local cold therapy can be used as an adjunct for pain relief during a gout flare. It should be applied safely and understood as supportive care rather than proof that the acute anti-inflammatory plan or long-term urate management is unnecessary.','Use safe local cold therapy as an adjunct during a treated gout flare.','inflammatory joint pain'),
q('psoriasis-impact-small-bsa','Psoriasis severity beyond surface area','Medicine','Dermatology',3,'psoriasis','A patient has psoriasis affecting a small skin area on the palms but cannot work because of fissuring and pain. Which severity consideration is most appropriate?',[
['Functional and psychosocial impact can be high despite small area','Sensitive-site disease can cause substantial disability beyond body-surface extent.'],['Small area guarantees trivial disease','An extent estimate does not capture site-specific functional burden.'],['Only the number of plaques matters','Plaque count does not measure effects on work or wellbeing.'],['Normal vital signs exclude treatment need','A chronic skin disorder can be disabling without vital-sign abnormalities.']], 'Psoriasis assessment includes extent, site and its effect on daily life. Palm disease may involve little body surface while causing major occupational disability, so clinical impact should influence care and referral rather than relying on area alone.','Include functional impact when assessing limited-area psoriasis at a disabling site.','cutaneous inflammation'),
q('psoriasis-arthritis-yearly-screen','Joint assessment in psoriasis','Medicine','Dermatology',2,'psoriasis','An adult has longstanding plaque psoriasis and no previously diagnosed arthritis. Which routine associated-disease assessment should be offered periodically?',[
['Assessment for psoriatic arthritis','Psoriasis can be accompanied by an inflammatory joint disease.'],['Only checking the color of scales','Skin appearance alone cannot identify joint symptoms or functional change.'],['Exclude arthritis because plaques are stable','Joint disease may occur independently of current skin severity.'],['Wait until irreversible deformity appears','Earlier identification supports timely specialist management.']], 'Psoriatic arthritis can develop in people with psoriasis, and skin stability does not reliably exclude joint involvement. Periodic assessment for joint symptoms and appropriate referral can identify disease before advanced functional loss or deformity.','Offer periodic psoriatic arthritis assessment in patients with psoriasis.','autoimmune joint pathology'),
q('psoriasis-new-inflammatory-joints','Psoriatic arthritis referral','Medicine','Musculoskeletal',4,'psoriasis','A patient with psoriasis develops persistent swollen finger joints, morning stiffness and a swollen whole digit. What is the most appropriate next action?',[
['Refer for rheumatological assessment of possible psoriatic arthritis','The inflammatory joint pattern warrants assessment for associated arthritis.'],['Treat only the skin and ignore joint symptoms','Topical skin treatment does not address a possible inflammatory arthritis.'],['Assume psoriasis excludes inflammatory joint disease','Psoriasis is associated with rather than protective against psoriatic arthritis.'],['Wait until all plaques disappear before assessment','The joint problem deserves its own timely evaluation.']], 'Persistent synovitis and dactylitis in a person with psoriasis raise concern for psoriatic arthritis. Timely rheumatological assessment addresses a potentially damaging joint disease; controlling plaques alone does not resolve the need to evaluate inflammatory musculoskeletal symptoms.','Refer psoriasis with persistent inflammatory joint symptoms for rheumatological assessment.','autoimmune joint pathology'),
q('generalized-pustular-psoriasis-urgent','Acute severe psoriasis referral','Medicine','Dermatology',4,'psoriasis','A patient with psoriasis develops generalized pustules, widespread inflamed skin and systemic illness. What level of assessment is appropriate?',[
['Immediate same-day specialist assessment','Generalized pustular psoriasis can require urgent systemic care.'],['Routine review after the next annual screening','Systemic illness with generalized pustules is not a routine stable presentation.'],['A topical moisturizer as the only plan','Supportive skin care alone does not address the potentially severe syndrome.'],['Telephone reassurance without assessing severity','The acute extent and systemic features require direct assessment.']], 'Generalized pustular psoriasis with systemic illness is an urgent presentation. Same-day specialist assessment is recommended because the extent and systemic burden differ fundamentally from an uncomplicated localized plaque flare and may require hospital-level management.','Arrange same-day specialist care for generalized pustular psoriasis.','cutaneous inflammation'),
q('erythrodermic-psoriasis-urgent','Erythroderma in psoriasis','Medicine','Dermatology',4,'psoriasis','A patient with chronic psoriasis becomes diffusely red and scaly over nearly the entire skin surface and feels shivery and weak. What is the best next action?',[
['Urgent same-day specialist assessment for erythroderma','Widespread inflammatory skin involvement can compromise systemic stability.'],['Continue a small-area topical plan without review','A localized maintenance regimen is inadequate for this major acute change.'],['Wait several months for spontaneous resolution','The extensive acute presentation carries potentially serious systemic risk.'],['Classify this as mild disease because there are no pustules','Severe erythroderma does not require pustules to be urgent.']], 'Erythroderma is a severe widespread skin presentation that can affect temperature regulation and fluid balance. In psoriasis it warrants immediate specialist assessment; absence of pustules does not make near-total skin inflammation a routine mild flare.','Recognize erythroderma as an urgent psoriasis presentation even without pustules.','skin barrier physiology'),
q('psoriasis-topical-nonresponse-adherence','Review before topical psoriasis escalation','Medicine','Dermatology',4,'psoriasis','A patient reports that topical psoriasis treatment has failed but says it is too greasy to use at work and he applies it only on weekends. What should be addressed before judging adequate treatment failure?',[
['Acceptability formulation and actual use of the topical regimen','A practical barrier to use can explain poor response.'],['Declare failure from the prescription record alone','A prescription does not establish adequate application.'],['Repeat the same advice without discussing the barrier','Ignoring acceptability leaves the identified adherence problem unresolved.'],['Move immediately to systemic treatment without reassessment','Treatment exposure and suitable formulation should first be reviewed.']], 'Topical treatment effectiveness depends on whether the regimen is used and is practical for the patient. Reviewing formulation, acceptability and actual application can address apparent failure and should precede escalation based solely on a prescription having been issued.','Review formulation and adherence barriers before escalating topical psoriasis treatment.','topical drug delivery'),
q('psoriasis-psychological-burden','Psychological impact in psoriasis','Medicine','Behavioral Health',3,'psoriasis','An adult with visible psoriasis avoids relationships and work meetings and reports persistent low mood. Skin extent is unchanged. What should the assessment include?',[
['Psychological and social impact including possible depression','Visible disease can carry substantial burden independent of changing extent.'],['Only a repeat body-surface area estimate','Extent alone does not capture mood and social functioning.'],['Assume emotional effects are unrelated to skin care','The disease impact should be assessed as part of comprehensive care.'],['Wait until the skin becomes more extensive','Current distress warrants attention without waiting for greater area.']], 'Psoriasis can affect mood, relationships and social participation even when the skin area is stable. Assessment should include these effects and possible depression, because treatment success is not defined solely by an unchanged or improved plaque count.','Assess depression and social impact when psoriasis limits participation.','depression severity formulation'),
q('psoriasis-cardiovascular-risk-assessment','Comorbidity review in severe psoriasis','Medicine','Cardiovascular',3,'psoriasis','A patient with severe psoriasis has not had cardiovascular risk reviewed for years. He feels well and focuses on his skin. Which additional assessment is appropriate?',[
['Review cardiovascular risk factors and prevention needs','Severe psoriasis care includes attention to cardiovascular comorbidity.'],['Assume skin-limited symptoms exclude vascular risk','Cardiovascular risk may be asymptomatic and independent of current skin discomfort.'],['Use a plaque count as a substitute for blood-pressure measurement','Skin extent cannot measure an individual cardiovascular risk factor.'],['Wait for chest pain before assessing prevention','Risk assessment is intended to act before a clinical event occurs.']], 'Severe psoriasis is a reason to consider associated cardiovascular risk and modifiable factors. Preventive assessment is relevant before symptoms develop and complements dermatological treatment, rather than relying on skin symptoms to signal systemic vascular risk.','Include cardiovascular risk review in severe psoriasis care.','chronic inflammation and vascular risk')
);
references.sickle=['NHLBI: Sickle cell disease health effects','https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sickle-cell-disease/health-effects'];
references.adrenalEmergency=['Society for Endocrinology: Adrenal crisis','https://www.endocrinology.org/clinical-practice/clinical-guidance/adrenal-crisis/'];
specs.push(
q('b12-neurological-without-macrocytosis','B12 deficiency without anemia','Medicine','Hematology',3,'b12','An adult with risk factors for B12 deficiency develops paresthesias and gait imbalance, but hemoglobin and mean cell volume are normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',[
['Normal blood counts do not exclude B12-related neurological disease','Neurological symptoms may occur without anemia or macrocytosis.'],['B12 deficiency is impossible without macrocytosis','Hematologic changes are not required in every deficient patient.'],['Normal hemoglobin rules out all nutritional neurological causes','A blood count does not assess every neurological consequence of deficiency.'],['The normal count proves a functional symptom disorder','Normal hematology does not establish that symptoms are functional.']], 'B12 deficiency should not be ruled out solely because anemia or macrocytosis is absent. Compatible neurological symptoms and risk factors remain relevant, so a normal blood count cannot substitute for an appropriate deficiency assessment.','Avoid excluding B12 deficiency from neurological symptoms solely because the blood count is normal.','megaloblastic anemia mechanism'),
q('b12-indeterminate-mma-next','Indeterminate B12 with symptoms','Medicine','Hematology',4,'b12','A symptomatic adult has a total B12 result in the laboratory indeterminate range. The clinical picture remains compatible with deficiency. Which additional biochemical test can help clarify the result?',[
['Serum methylmalonic acid','MMA can provide functional evidence when the initial B12 result is indeterminate.'],['D-dimer','This assesses fibrin turnover rather than B12-dependent metabolism.'],['Serum lipase','A pancreatic enzyme does not clarify an indeterminate B12 concentration.'],['Troponin as the routine confirmatory test','Cardiac injury testing does not assess functional cobalamin status.']], 'An indeterminate total B12 concentration may not settle a compatible symptomatic presentation. Methylmalonic acid can help assess functional deficiency within the laboratory pathway, while results still need context because renal impairment and other factors can affect interpretation.','Use MMA to help clarify symptomatic indeterminate B12 results.','B12-dependent metabolism'),
q('nitrous-oxide-functional-b12-assessment','Functional B12 inactivation','Medicine','Nervous System',4,'b12','A patient with heavy recreational nitrous oxide exposure develops sensory ataxia. Total B12 is not clearly low. Which testing approach is particularly relevant to suspected functional deficiency?',[
['Methylmalonic acid or homocysteine testing','Functional markers can reveal B12 pathway disruption despite a misleading total level.'],['Exclude B12-related disease from total concentration alone','Nitrous oxide can impair B12 function without a clearly low total value.'],['Use serum sodium as the only neurological investigation','Sodium does not directly assess the suspected cobalamin pathway defect.'],['Measure only fasting cholesterol','Cholesterol does not evaluate nitrous oxide-associated functional deficiency.']], 'Nitrous oxide can inactivate B12 function, so the total circulating concentration may not reflect functional availability. Compatible neurological symptoms warrant a functional-marker approach and clinical management rather than exclusion based solely on a non-low total B12 result.','Assess functional B12 markers in suspected nitrous oxide-related neurological disease.','cobalamin metabolism'),
q('b12-neurological-treatment-not-delay','Prompt treatment of suspected B12 neurological disease','Medicine','Nervous System',4,'b12','An adult has suspected megaloblastic anemia with progressive gait and sensory abnormalities suggestive of B12-related neurological disease. Blood has been drawn for testing. Should treatment await all results?',[
['Do not delay indicated B12 replacement while awaiting results','Progressive neurological involvement warrants prompt treatment.'],['Wait several months for spontaneous improvement','Delay may allow further neurological harm.'],['Treat only with folate and ignore possible B12 deficiency','Folate alone does not address B12-related neurological injury.'],['Use the blood count as the only future neurological follow-up','Neurological progression requires clinical assessment beyond hematology.']], 'When suspected B12 deficiency is accompanied by megaloblastic anemia and neurological symptoms, replacement should not be delayed for pending results. Obtaining appropriate samples is useful, but avoiding further neurological injury takes priority over completing every etiological test first.','Avoid delaying replacement for suspected B12 deficiency with progressive neurological involvement.','megaloblastic anemia mechanism'),
q('b12-autoimmune-gastritis-antibody','Investigating the cause of B12 deficiency','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'b12','A patient has confirmed B12 deficiency and no dietary explanation. The history suggests autoimmune gastritis. Which test can support that suspected cause?',[
['Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies','A positive result supports autoimmune gastritis as an absorption-related cause.'],['Anti-TSH receptor antibodies','These support Graves disease rather than intrinsic-factor-related B12 malabsorption.'],['D-dimer','This does not identify autoimmune gastric impairment of B12 absorption.'],['Urine culture','This evaluates urinary infection rather than the cause of cobalamin deficiency.']], 'After confirming B12 deficiency, identifying the cause guides long-term treatment. Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies can support autoimmune gastritis, distinguishing an absorption problem from an inadequate dietary supply, although the test has limitations and is not the entire evaluation.','Use anti-intrinsic factor antibodies to support autoimmune gastritis in confirmed B12 deficiency.','intrinsic factor B12 transport'),
q('b12-negative-if-antibody-limits','Limits of intrinsic factor antibody testing','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',4,'b12','A patient has confirmed B12 deficiency and a clinical picture strongly suggesting autoimmune gastritis, but anti-intrinsic factor antibodies are negative. Which interpretation is correct?',[
['A negative antibody result does not exclude autoimmune gastritis','The test can be negative despite the suspected absorption disorder.'],['Autoimmune gastritis is permanently excluded','Limited sensitivity prevents that absolute conclusion.'],['The result proves dietary deficiency','A negative etiological test does not establish a different cause by itself.'],['No replacement is needed because the antibody is negative','Confirmed deficiency still requires an appropriate treatment plan.']], 'Anti-intrinsic factor antibody testing can support autoimmune gastritis when positive but is not sufficiently sensitive to exclude it in every negative case. Persistent clinical suspicion may justify additional assessment, while treatment of the established deficiency remains necessary.','Recognize that negative intrinsic factor antibodies do not exclude autoimmune gastritis.','intrinsic factor B12 transport'),
q('b12-autoimmune-lifelong-replacement','Long-term B12 replacement with irreversible malabsorption','Medicine','Hematology',4,'b12','A patient with B12 deficiency caused by autoimmune gastritis improves after intramuscular replacement and asks whether treatment can now stop permanently. What is the best explanation?',[
['Ongoing lifelong replacement is needed for the persistent cause','Symptom correction does not restore the underlying absorption mechanism.'],['Improvement proves the autoimmune process has disappeared','Clinical response does not demonstrate recovery of intrinsic-factor-related absorption.'],['Replacement is needed only when anemia becomes severe again','Waiting for recurrent deficiency risks renewed neurological or hematologic harm.'],['A normal post-treatment level always permits permanent cessation','A repleted concentration reflects treatment rather than removal of the cause.']], 'Autoimmune gastritis is a persistent malabsorptive cause of B12 deficiency. Replacement corrects the deficiency but does not itself reverse the cause, so lifelong replacement is required rather than waiting for symptoms or severe anemia to recur.','Explain lifelong replacement when autoimmune gastritis causes B12 deficiency.','intrinsic factor B12 transport'),
q('b12-total-gastrectomy-replacement-plan','B12 care after total gastrectomy','Medicine','Gastrointestinal',3,'b12','An adult has undergone total gastrectomy and feels well several months later. What should the long-term plan include regarding vitamin B12?',[
['Lifelong B12 replacement for the irreversible malabsorptive risk','Total gastrectomy permanently removes essential gastric support for absorption.'],['No replacement unless diarrhea develops','B12 depletion can occur without diarrhea.'],['A normal early level guarantees lifelong sufficiency','Body stores can delay deficiency despite permanent malabsorption.'],['Only short-term oral iron regardless of B12 status','Iron does not replace cobalamin or its neurological functions.']], 'Total gastrectomy creates an irreversible risk of B12 malabsorption even before stores are exhausted. A normal early concentration or absence of symptoms does not establish future sufficiency, so lifelong replacement planning is needed rather than waiting for depletion to become clinically apparent.','Plan lifelong B12 replacement after total gastrectomy before delayed depletion occurs.','intrinsic factor B12 transport'),
q('adrenal-crisis-treat-before-confirm','Suspected adrenal crisis emergency','Medicine','Endocrine',4,'adrenalEmergency','A patient with known adrenal insufficiency presents after vomiting with hypotension and marked weakness. Adrenal crisis is suspected. What should happen while confirmatory investigations are arranged?',[
['Give emergency hydrocortisone and fluid resuscitation without delay','A suspected crisis is life-threatening and treatment must not await confirmation.'],['Wait for a stimulation test before any treatment','Diagnostic delay can worsen an immediately dangerous deficiency.'],['Give only an antiemetic and discharge','Vomiting and hypotension may indicate a systemic adrenal emergency.'],['Withhold fluids solely until every hormone result returns','Resuscitation should not be delayed for confirmatory testing.']], 'Suspected adrenal crisis requires immediate glucocorticoid treatment and appropriate fluid resuscitation. Investigations can help establish the cause, but delaying treatment until confirmation risks deterioration; known insufficiency with vomiting and hypotension supplies a compelling emergency context.','Treat suspected adrenal crisis immediately rather than awaiting confirmatory tests.','adrenal hormone physiology'),
q('adrenal-sick-day-education','Stress dosing education in adrenal insufficiency','Medicine','Endocrine',3,'adrenal','A patient newly diagnosed with adrenal insufficiency asks whether the usual replacement schedule is sufficient during every febrile illness. What education should be provided?',[
['An individualized sick-day and emergency management plan','Physiological stress can require increased glucocorticoid replacement.'],['Never change the plan during any illness','Patients may be unable to mount the usual cortisol stress response.'],['Stop replacement whenever appetite falls','Illness can increase rather than eliminate steroid requirements.'],['Take extra mineral supplements as the only stress response','Supplements do not replace the needed glucocorticoid stress response.']], 'People with adrenal insufficiency cannot reliably increase endogenous cortisol during physiological stress. They therefore need explicit sick-day rules and an emergency plan, including when to seek help, rather than assuming that the usual maintenance schedule fits every acute illness.','Provide sick-day and emergency instructions for physiological stress in adrenal insufficiency.','cortisol stress response'),
q('adrenal-vomiting-oral-route-failure','Vomiting during adrenal replacement','Medicine','Endocrine',4,'adrenal','An adult with adrenal insufficiency has repeated vomiting and cannot retain oral replacement or fluids. Which concern most strongly supports urgent medical assessment?',[
['Oral glucocorticoid delivery may fail during a high-stress illness','Parenteral replacement and fluid assessment may be needed.'],['Vomiting reliably increases absorption of every oral dose','The drug may not be retained long enough for adequate absorption.'],['No steroid is needed if the patient is not eating','Glucocorticoid needs persist independently of meal intake.'],['A normal prior outpatient cortisol removes current risk','Past stability does not prevent crisis during impaired replacement.']], 'Repeated vomiting can make oral replacement unreliable precisely when stress may raise glucocorticoid needs. Urgent assessment is required for parenteral replacement and hydration, rather than relying on an oral plan the patient cannot retain or on previous stability.','Escalate repeated vomiting that prevents oral replacement in adrenal insufficiency.','cortisol stress response'),
q('adrenal-emergency-card-preparedness','Emergency information for adrenal insufficiency','Medicine','General Principles',2,'adrenal','An adult with adrenal insufficiency is stable on replacement. What practical preparation helps unfamiliar clinicians recognize emergency steroid needs during future illness?',[
['Carry appropriate steroid emergency information and understand the crisis plan','Clear emergency information supports timely recognition and treatment.'],['Hide the diagnosis to avoid unnecessary questions','Unfamiliar staff need to know the risk during acute illness.'],['Rely only on recalling the dose while severely unwell','Illness may impair the patient ability to communicate essential details.'],['Stop follow-up once an emergency card is issued','The card complements rather than replaces ongoing clinical care.']], 'Emergency information helps clinicians recognize adrenal risk when a patient is acutely unwell or unable to give a history. It should accompany education about replacement and crisis care, because documentation alone cannot replace understanding the plan or routine follow-up.','Use steroid emergency information as part of adrenal crisis preparedness.','medical safety communication'),
q('adrenal-longterm-steroid-withdrawal','Adrenal risk after prolonged glucocorticoids','Medicine','Endocrine',4,'adrenal','A patient has taken systemic glucocorticoids for many months and wants to stop abruptly because the original inflammatory disease is controlled. What issue must be considered?',[
['Suppressed adrenal function and the need for a supervised withdrawal plan','Prolonged exposure can impair endogenous cortisol reserve.'],['Disease control proves normal adrenal reserve','Control of the original illness does not establish recovery of the adrenal axis.'],['Abrupt cessation is always safer than tapering','Sudden withdrawal can expose a patient with suppressed reserve to deficiency.'],['Only the current pain score determines withdrawal safety','Adrenal risk depends on exposure and physiological recovery as well.']], 'Long-term systemic glucocorticoid exposure can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis. Improvement of the original inflammatory disorder does not prove adequate endogenous cortisol reserve, so withdrawal requires a supervised individualized plan rather than abrupt cessation.','Assess adrenal suppression risk before withdrawing prolonged systemic glucocorticoids.','glucocorticoid negative feedback'),
q('adrenal-morning-cortisol-timing','Initial testing for suspected adrenal insufficiency','Medicine','Endocrine',3,'adrenal','A stable outpatient has symptoms suggesting adrenal insufficiency and is being assessed using the guideline morning cortisol pathway. Why is an 8-to-9 am sample preferable to an arbitrary late-evening sample?',[
['The recommended pathway uses a standardized morning cortisol assessment','Diurnal variation makes sampling time important for interpretation.'],['Cortisol has no diurnal variation','Normal cortisol secretion varies through the day.'],['Evening testing always gives the highest normal value','Evening values are usually lower rather than the expected morning peak.'],['Sampling time independently proves the diagnosis','Standardized timing improves interpretation but does not alone diagnose disease.']], 'Cortisol normally varies with time of day, so a diagnostic pathway specifies morning sampling. An arbitrary evening value cannot be interpreted as though it were obtained under that standardized pathway, and the result must still be assessed in the clinical and medication context.','Use standardized morning timing when applying an initial cortisol diagnostic pathway.','circadian endocrine physiology'),
q('adrenal-replacement-overexposure-review','Possible glucocorticoid overreplacement','Medicine','Endocrine',3,'adrenal','A patient on adrenal replacement develops progressive weight gain, skin thinning and worsening glucose control. What aspect of care should be reviewed alongside other possible causes?',[
['Possible glucocorticoid overreplacement','Excess replacement exposure can produce metabolic and cutaneous features.'],['Assume the replacement dose can never cause adverse effects','Replacement still requires clinical monitoring for excess and deficiency.'],['Increase the dose automatically because weight rose','Escalation could worsen excess glucocorticoid exposure.'],['Stop every steroid immediately without supervision','Abrupt cessation can be dangerous in adrenal insufficiency.']], 'Replacement dosing aims to supply physiological needs, but excessive exposure can still cause weight gain, skin changes and metabolic effects. The clinical pattern warrants review of dose and other causes while preserving safe replacement rather than automatic escalation or abrupt cessation.','Review possible overreplacement when adrenal therapy is accompanied by glucocorticoid-excess features.','cortisol metabolic actions'),
q('adrenal-underreplacement-clinical-review','Possible inadequate adrenal replacement','Medicine','Endocrine',4,'adrenal','A patient on adrenal replacement has persistent weight loss, nausea and postural dizziness despite reporting regular doses. What is the best next approach?',[
['Review replacement adequacy adherence absorption and other causes','Symptoms may reflect inadequate exposure but are not specific enough for blind adjustment.'],['Assume every symptom proves a need for unlimited dose increases','Symptoms require contextual assessment because overreplacement also causes harm.'],['Exclude adrenal contribution because treatment was prescribed','A prescription does not guarantee an adequate physiological exposure.'],['Stop all replacement to clarify symptoms without supervision','Withdrawing essential replacement can precipitate serious deficiency.']], 'Weight loss, nausea and postural symptoms can suggest inadequate replacement, but they also have other causes. A structured clinical review should assess actual dosing, illness and absorption while avoiding both unassessed dose escalation and unsafe withdrawal of necessary glucocorticoid support.','Reassess exposure and alternative causes when symptoms suggest inadequate adrenal replacement.','cortisol and circulatory support'),
q('aplastic-trilineage-cytopenia-pattern','Marrow failure clinical pattern','Medicine','Hematology',3,'aplastic','An adult has fatigue, recurrent infections and easy bruising. The blood count shows anemia, neutropenia and thrombocytopenia. Which broad process could explain all three findings?',[
['Bone marrow failure affecting multiple cell lineages','Reduced production can link anemia infection risk and bleeding.'],['Isolated dietary iron deficiency alone','An isolated red-cell deficiency does not best explain all three low cell lines.'],['Pure peripheral nerve compression','A focal neuropathy does not produce pancytopenia.'],['An isolated coagulation factor deficiency','A clotting factor problem does not explain neutropenia and anemia together.']], 'The combination of anemia, neutropenia and thrombocytopenia points to a multilineage production or other pancytopenic process. Aplastic marrow failure is one important possibility requiring specialist evaluation, unlike an isolated red-cell or clotting-factor disorder.','Recognize multilineage marrow failure as a unifying explanation for fatigue infection and bruising.','hematopoiesis'),
q('aplastic-medication-exposure-history','Exposure history in suspected marrow failure','Medicine','Hematology',3,'aplastic','A patient with newly identified pancytopenia is being evaluated for possible aplastic anemia. Which history is particularly important when looking for a potentially reversible contributor?',[
['Recent medicines chemotherapy and toxic exposures','Some drugs and toxins can damage blood-forming stem cells.'],['Only preferred exercise music','This does not identify a recognized marrow-toxic exposure.'],['Only refractive error history','Visual correction does not explain a new multilineage marrow problem.'],['Ignore prescriptions because all approved drugs are harmless','Medicines can still cause clinically important marrow toxicity.']], 'Aplastic anemia may be acquired after medication or toxic injury to hematopoietic stem cells. An exposure history is therefore important alongside the broader workup and can identify an avoidable contributor; pancytopenia should not be assigned a cause from symptoms alone.','Take a marrow-toxic medication and exposure history in suspected aplastic anemia.','hematopoiesis'),
q('aplastic-bone-marrow-diagnostic-assessment','Evaluating suspected aplastic anemia','Medicine','Hematology',4,'aplastic','An adult has unexplained persistent pancytopenia and low blood-cell production is suspected. Which assessment most directly helps determine whether the marrow is failing to produce adequate cells?',[
['Specialist blood and bone marrow assessment','Marrow examination can characterize cellular production and competing diagnoses.'],['A single stool occult-blood test as definitive proof','Blood loss alone does not explain all low cell lineages.'],['A normal chest radiograph as definitive exclusion','Chest imaging does not directly assess marrow cellularity.'],['Only serum sodium testing','Sodium does not characterize hematopoietic production.']], 'Persistent pancytopenia requires investigation of marrow production and other causes. Specialist blood and bone marrow assessment can distinguish aplastic failure from alternative marrow disorders, whereas unrelated normal tests cannot establish or exclude the diagnosis.','Use specialist marrow assessment to investigate unexplained persistent pancytopenia.','hematopoiesis'),
q('aplastic-neutropenia-infection-risk','Infection risk in marrow failure','Medicine','Hematology',3,'aplastic','A patient with aplastic anemia asks why fever needs prompt attention even when fatigue and hemoglobin are unchanged. Which explanation is best?',[
['Low neutrophil production can impair defense against infection','Infection risk is determined by more than the red-cell count.'],['A stable hemoglobin guarantees normal immune defense','Hemoglobin does not measure neutrophil number or function.'],['Bruising is the only possible complication','Marrow failure can affect white cells as well as platelets.'],['Every fever must be caused by iron deficiency','Iron deficiency does not explain all fever in an immunocompromised patient.']], 'Aplastic anemia involves more than red cells and may reduce infection-fighting white cells. Consequently, stable hemoglobin does not demonstrate immune safety; fever can signal an important infection and needs prompt clinical attention in the setting of marrow failure.','Explain infection risk from low white-cell production despite stable hemoglobin.','hematopoiesis'),
q('sickle-new-infiltrate-acute-chest','Acute chest syndrome recognition','Medicine','Respiratory',4,'sickle','An adult with sickle cell disease admitted for pain develops fever, falling oxygen saturation and a new pulmonary infiltrate. Which complication needs urgent evaluation?',[
['Acute chest syndrome','New infiltrate with respiratory illness in sickle cell disease is an emergency.'],['Uncomplicated limb pain alone','New hypoxemia and lung findings indicate an additional complication.'],['Stable chronic anemia without acute change','A new infiltrate and fever are not explained by baseline anemia alone.'],['Isolated allergic rhinitis','Upper-airway allergy does not explain the new pulmonary infiltrate.']], 'A new pulmonary infiltrate with fever or respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease raises acute chest syndrome. It can develop during a pain admission and requires urgent assessment rather than assuming all deterioration belongs to uncomplicated vaso-occlusive limb pain.','Recognize acute chest syndrome when a pain admission develops hypoxemia and an infiltrate.','sickle hemoglobin polymerization'),
q('sickle-aplastic-crisis-reticulocytopenia','Acute anemia with low reticulocytes in sickle disease','Medicine','Hematology',3,'sickle','A patient with sickle cell disease develops a marked hemoglobin fall after a viral illness. Reticulocytes are very low compared with baseline. Which complication best fits?',[
['Transient aplastic crisis','Suppression of red-cell production produces an inappropriately low reticulocyte count.'],['Compensated hemolysis with an appropriate marrow response','Effective compensation would usually increase reticulocyte production.'],['Isolated painful bone infarction without hematologic change','This does not account for marked anemia and low reticulocytes.'],['Normal sickle baseline by definition','A large change with reticulocytopenia requires assessment of acute production failure.']], 'People with chronic hemolysis depend on continuing marrow production. A marked hemoglobin fall with unexpectedly low reticulocytes after a viral illness suggests transient aplastic crisis, commonly associated with parvovirus B19, rather than a well-compensated increase in red-cell destruction.','Use reticulocytopenia to identify an aplastic crisis during sickle cell acute anemia.','sickle hemoglobin polymerization'),
q('sickle-focal-neurology-emergency','Acute neurological deficit in sickle disease','Medicine','Nervous System',4,'sickle','An adult with sickle cell disease develops sudden unilateral weakness and speech difficulty during a pain episode. What is the most appropriate interpretation?',[
['Possible stroke requiring emergency assessment','Sickle cell disease can cause cerebrovascular complications that need urgent evaluation.'],['An expected harmless component of every pain crisis','New focal neurological deficits are not routine uncomplicated pain features.'],['A reason to wait until analgesia has completely worn off','Delay risks missing a time-sensitive neurological emergency.'],['A diagnosis of peripheral joint inflammation alone','Joint inflammation cannot explain aphasia with unilateral weakness.']], 'Sickle cell disease increases the risk of neurological vascular complications. Sudden focal weakness and language difficulty therefore require emergency stroke assessment, even during a familiar pain admission; the existing pain diagnosis should not obscure a new cerebral event.','Escalate sudden focal neurological deficits in sickle disease as possible stroke.','ischemic stroke localization'),
q('sickle-priapism-urgent-assessment','Vaso-occlusive urological complication','Medicine','Hematology',4,'sickle','A man with sickle cell disease has a painful erection lasting five hours without sexual stimulation. What response is most appropriate?',[
['Urgent emergency assessment for ischemic priapism','A prolonged painful erection can threaten erectile tissue viability.'],['Routine review at the next annual appointment','Prolonged pain and duration require a much more urgent assessment.'],['Assume this is a harmless sign of improved circulation','Sickle-associated obstruction can compromise rather than improve tissue perfusion.'],['Treat only baseline anemia and ignore the local symptom','The acute urological complication requires direct evaluation.']], 'Sickle cell disease can produce priapism through impaired local circulation. A painful prolonged erection is a time-sensitive emergency because sustained ischemia can damage erectile tissue; it should not be deferred as a routine chronic anemia issue.','Recognize prolonged painful priapism in sickle disease as an emergency.','sickle hemoglobin polymerization'),
q('sickle-chronic-transfusion-iron-review','Iron burden after repeated transfusion','Medicine','Hematology',3,'sickle','An adult with sickle cell disease has received repeated red-cell transfusions over several years. What cumulative complication should be assessed as part of ongoing care?',[
['Transfusional iron overload','Repeated transfusions can add iron beyond the body ability to excrete it.'],['Guaranteed iron depletion from every transfusion','Transfused red cells deliver iron rather than removing it.'],['Only immediate allergy with no cumulative risks','Long-term exposure has risks beyond acute transfusion reactions.'],['No follow-up if the last transfusion was uneventful','An uneventful single episode does not exclude cumulative iron accumulation.']], 'Repeated red-cell transfusions add iron to the body, which has limited capacity for regulated iron excretion. Long-term follow-up therefore includes assessment of iron burden even when individual transfusions caused no immediate reaction, distinguishing cumulative toxicity from acute transfusion safety.','Assess cumulative iron overload after repeated transfusions in sickle cell disease.','iron overload diagnostic pattern')
);
specs.push(q('hypertension-renal-target-organ-assessment','Renal target-organ assessment in hypertension','Medicine','Renal',3,'bp','An adult has newly confirmed hypertension and no urinary symptoms. Which urine test contributes to assessment of renal target-organ damage?',[
['Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio','Albuminuria can identify renal involvement even without urinary symptoms.'],['Urine culture as the only renal screen','A culture assesses infection rather than quantifying albumin excretion.'],['Urine ketones as the sole chronic renal-damage marker','Ketones do not directly assess albuminuric kidney damage.'],['Urine pregnancy testing in every adult as the only renal assessment','Pregnancy testing does not characterize hypertensive renal injury.']], 'Hypertension can produce or coexist with kidney damage before urinary symptoms appear. Measuring urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio helps assess albuminuria and renal risk, complementing renal blood tests rather than substituting an infection test for target-organ assessment.','Assess albuminuria as part of renal target-organ evaluation in confirmed hypertension.','glomerular filtration physiology'));
